% bbdd(8) | Linux

NAME
====

`bbdd` - BPF-based BFD dataplane daemon

SYNOPSIS
========

`bbdd -V`

`bbdd [-v | -q] [-N] [-t | --timestamp] [--json] [--sockdir <PATH>]
{start | stop | echo | session | global | bfdd | monitor | help}`

DESCRIPTION
===========

`bbdd` is a daemon that implements the BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding
Detection, [RFC 5880][]) dataplane in-kernel using the Linux BPF subsystem.

The daemon can be controlled by one of two ways:

  - through a [JSON RPC][] based protocol, by `bbdd` CLI interface.
    In this role `bbdd` serves as a fully independent BFD service.

  - through the FRR BFD daemon wire protocol. In that role, `bbdd` serves as
    a BFD datapath daemon.

    The `bfdd` suite of command manages the BFD daemon connection. See
    COMMANDS below.

## Method of Operation

The daemon manages BFD sessions, each of which monitors the bidirectional
forwarding path between two endpoints.

To transmit BFD packets, `bbdd` creates a pair of VETH netdevices
configured with `mq` and `fq` qdiscs. By looping BFD packets from TX end of
the VETH pair back to the RX end, and setting an appropriate timestamp at
the SKB, sending packets in regular intervals is effected.

To manage receive side, `bbdd` maintains a per-session timer that is reset
regularly as packets for that session arrive.

This way the full BFD receive and transmit cycle is offloaded to the
kernel. `bbdd` userspace daemon then takes care of managing exceptional
states: timeouts, user-issued configuration changes, session state machine
and other more complex details. Meanwhile the regular, stable state is
handled by the in-kernel BPF programs.

OPTIONS
=======

`-v, --verbose, -q, --quiet`

: Be more or less verbose, these options compensate each other. When just
  `-q` is given, show no output (beyond command line parsing), not even
  error messages. When `-v` show debug messages in addition to error
  messages.

`-N`

: Suppress human-readable unit conversion. Intervals and times are
  shown as raw microseconds rather than formatted strings such as
  `100ms`.

`-t, --timestamp`

: Prefix monitor notifications with a timestamp.

`--sockdir <PATH>`

: Location of the directory where Unix sockets used to communicate with the
  daemon are put.

  By default, `bbdd` places the socket in a directory @DEFAULT_SOCKDIR@.
  The `--sockdir` option overrides the compiled-in default.

`--json`

: Instead of formatting the daemon's response in a human-readable way,
  print the raw JSON result object. Errors are still printed to
  standard error as usual.

COMMANDS
========

`start`

: Start the daemon.

`stop`

: Stop the running daemon.

`echo`

: Check liveness of the daemon and measure one-way JRPC latency — the time
  it takes to accept the echo request over the socket, parse it, dispatch it
  and act on it.

`session`

: Manage BFD sessions. See `bbdd-session(8)` for further details.

`bfdd`

: Manage the connection to a BFD daemon (BFDD). See `bbdd-bfdd(8)` for
  further details.

`global diag stats`

: Show global dagnostic statistics. There are currently no global
  statistics besides diagnostic ones.

`monitor`

: Subscribe to daemon monitoring topics and print received notifications.
  Available topics:

    `ringbuf`
    : Shows incoming BPF ring buffer events

    `bfddi`, `bfddo`
    : Respectively incoming and outgoing BFD daemon wire protocol messages

    `jrpc`
    : Incoming JSON RPC requests

    `session`
    : Session state change events

    `error`, `debug`
    : Runtime errors and debug messages emitted by the daemon. Even in a
      `-q` mode, errors and debug messages are still emitted through the
      monitor and can be observed that way.

    `all`
    : A special keyword that selects all topics.

  When no topics are given, the default is all topics except of `debug`.

